## 🏆 Core Project Insights & Operational Conclusion

### 1. The Accuracy Fallacy Exposed
The **Random Forest Classifier** achieved the highest raw test Accuracy at **91.66%**. However, it yielded an abysmal minority class Recall of just **13.16%** and an F1-Score of **0.1351**. This proves that the model simply favored the majority non-stroke class, making it completely unsafe for clinical diagnosis. 

### 2. Ensemble System Breakdown
- **Soft-Voting Ensemble:** Consolidated probability boundaries by computing a mathematical average of predictions from the top three models, achieving a strong **0.8084 ROC-AUC**.
- **Bayesian Ensemble:** Fed baseline outputs into a stacked array using a Gaussian Naive Bayes meta-learner, maximizing the classification landscape with a peak project F1-Score of **0.2304**.

### 3. Production Deployment Selection
The **Logistic Regression** architecture is definitively selected as the optimal model for production environment integration. In clinical stroke warning software, the operational cost of a False Negative (missing an endangered patient) is vastly higher than a False Positive (triggering a false alarm for additional testing). 

By successfully catching **81.58%** of real patient stroke occurrences (highest Recall) and maximizing boundary discrimination (**0.8250 ROC-AUC**), Logistic Regression minimizes life-threatening risk leakage, providing the robust diagnostic safety margin required for real-world deployment.

